            "pageCount": 1,
            "processed": true,
            "summary": "This document reports a case of severe contact dermatitis in a 60-year-old woman who used Cortivex Cream for eczema. The patient applied the cream to a small area on her forearm and experienced intense redness, swelling, and blistering within one day. The reaction was severe enough that she stopped using the cream, and the symptoms resolved over 10 days with the help of topical corticosteroid treatment. Further investigation revealed that the tube of Cortivex Cream she used was from a batch that had been recalled due to a manufacturing defect.\n\nThis document is relevant to patient safety, as it details a serious adverse reaction associated with a specific product linked to a quality issue. It highlights the importance of monitoring products for defects that can lead to harmful outcomes. The case illustrates the need for vigilance in quality assurance processes and safeguarding public health when it comes to dermatological products. The information may prompt further investigation into the circumstances surrounding the recall and its implications for other users of the product."
        }
